  "prompt_text": "This figure compares a normal mosquito-based food web with one involving genetically engineered mosquitoes carrying a lethality gene. In both panels, mosquitoes use a small pond for their aquatic stages, with eggs and larvae developing in the water and adults emerging and moving through the surrounding habitat. Birds perched in a nearby tree appear to feed on adult mosquitoes, while fallen leaves from the tree enter the pond and support the aquatic part of the system, linking terrestrial and aquatic processes.\n\nThe left panel shows the baseline cycle: adults lay eggs, larvae grow in the pond, and adults become prey for birds, suggesting a flow of energy between the pond and the tree-associated bird community. The right panel overlays this with modified mosquitoes marked by a red dot. Dashed arrows and the red markers indicate the spread of the engineered trait through adult, egg, and larval stages. The main visible message is that releasing engineered mosquitoes may alter not just mosquito reproduction, but also ecological connections across life stages and habitats, especially predator-prey links between aquatic breeding sites and insect-eating birds.",
